      "role": "user",
      "content": "i get the aging thing, but aging doesn't explain why the timing of his muscle movements feels so... synthetic. like, there are moments where the whole face moves in perfect unison, which is actually impossible for a human. i was reading this thread about how high-end deepfakes or even realistic bio-simulations are being used to maintain celebrity \"brands\" when the actual person can no longer handle the lifestyle. if you look at the way his jaw moves compared to his eyes, it's like two different layers are being rendered. do you think a high-fidelity digital or synthetic substitute could actually pass for a human in a low-res or mid-range video setting? it's starting to feel less like \"evolution\" and more like a replacement.",
